Z05-P01 | Taxonomic Workflow: Separating Recognition from Consumption | New manuscript (Z05 P19-B qualified workflow)
objective: Establish multi-character botanical and fungal identification gates while keeping all consumption decisions strictly withheld pending qualified expert review.
applies: All wild plant, berry, root, tree, and mushroom study across Minnesota and the Upper Midwest.
equipment: Regional taxonomic key, Bell Museum Atlas reference, metric ruler, notebook, camera.
diagram: Verify accepted binomial & voucher | Confirm multiple diagnostic traits | Keep consumption on permanent HOLD
visual: Conceptual four-view specimen voucher workflow separating morphological observation from withheld consumption clearance.
action: Verify each candidate species by accepted Latin binomial, taxonomic authority, synonyms, and regional Bell Museum Minnesota Biodiversity Atlas occurrence rather than ambiguous common names. @B01,B02,B03
action: Confirm multiple independent morphological characters at the observed life stage and cross-check every regional poisonous lookalike before recording a provisional field identification. @B20,B24
action: Treat species recognition and suitability for consumption as completely separate decisions; generated diagrams, single photos, and unreviewed draft profiles never authorize eating wild species. @B10,B21,B24
failure: Relying on common names, phone-app guesses, or single-photo matches causes lethal plant and mushroom poisonings. @B02,B06,B24
verify: Accepted binomial, multi-character traits, contrasting toxic lookalikes, and explicit consumption HOLD status are recorded together. @B03,B20,B24
stop: Stop identification immediately if any required diagnostic structure is missing, damaged, or inconsistent; call 1-800-222-1222 for any suspected toxic ingestion. @B09,B20,B24
reviewer: Regional botanist, mycologist, and medical toxicologist

Z05-P02 | Accepted Scientific Names, Authorities and Synonym Tracking | New manuscript (Z05 P19-C taxonomy)
objective: Eliminate dangerous common-name collisions by anchoring every species record to accepted Latin binomials, authorities, and verified synonyms.
applies: Cross-referencing regional field guides, USDA PLANTS, ITIS, Bell Museum Atlas, and MyCoPortal.
equipment: USDA PLANTS / ITIS / MyCoPortal taxonomic reference, notebook.
diagram: Anchor to Latin binomial & author | Track regional legacy synonyms | Reject ambiguous common names
visual: Conceptual nomenclature gate separating verified Latin binomials from ambiguous overlapping common names.
action: Record accepted Latin binomial, naming authority, and key historical synonyms for every profile because common names like hemlock, nightshade, elder, or wild carrot span unrelated safe and lethal taxa. @B03,B04
action: Keep plant taxonomy (USDA PLANTS, Bell Museum Herbarium) strictly separate from fungal taxonomy (MyCoPortal, Bell Museum Fungarium) because naming rules and revisions diverge. @B02,B03,B05
action: Log the exact taxonomic reference date on every voucher sheet because genetic studies periodically split familiar species complexes. @B04,B05
failure: Confusing Eastern hemlock conifer (Tsuga) with lethal water hemlock (Cicuta) or poison hemlock (Conium) through common names risks fatal ingestion. @B03,B11
verify: Every species profile lists accepted binomial, authority, synonym notes, and separate Plantae or Fungi schema. @B03,B04,B05
stop: Stop relying on any plant or mushroom note that supplies only a folk common name without a verified Latin binomial. @B03,B04
reviewer: Plant and fungal taxonomist

Z05-P03 | Herbarium and Fungarium Voucher Specimen Verification | New manuscript (Z05 P19-C voucher standards)
objective: Use curated museum vouchers and regional occurrence atlases properly without mistaking county presence maps for field identification.
applies: Corroborating Minnesota plant and fungal records through Bell Museum and MyCoPortal collections.
equipment: Bell Museum Biodiversity Atlas, MyCoPortal voucher data, field notes.
diagram: Check regional county occurrence | Compare traits to vouchered keys | Reject crowd-sourced app certainty
visual: Conceptual workflow connecting field multi-view notes to Bell Museum herbarium/fungarium voucher comparison and curator review.
action: Use Bell Museum Minnesota Biodiversity Atlas and MyCoPortal voucher records to confirm whether a candidate taxon actually occurs in Minnesota habitats and seasons. @B01,B02,B05
action: Corroborate observed diagnostic traits against curated specimen descriptions and dichotomous regional keys rather than image-search labels or automated phone apps. @B02,B20
action: Recognize that a museum occurrence dot confirms regional presence only—never that a specific roadside or forest specimen matches that taxon or is safe to eat. @B01,B02,B24
failure: Trusting an automated phone identification app or web search thumbnail causes misidentification of cryptic toxic mushrooms and plants. @B02,B06,B24
verify: County distribution, habitat fit, dichotomous key path, and curator review holds are recorded together. @B01,B02
stop: Stop identification when a field specimen falls outside documented regional distribution or contradicts vouchered keys. @B01,B02
reviewer: Herbarium and fungarium curator

Z05-P04 | Botanical Morphology: Leaf Arrangement, Margins and Venation | New manuscript (Z05 P19-C plant morphology)
objective: Read core vegetative anatomy—opposite, alternate, whorled, simple, and compound leaves—accurately before consulting regional keys.
applies: Field observation of woody shrubs, trees, vines, and herbaceous stems.
equipment: 10x hand lens, metric ruler, regional flora glossary.
diagram: Check opposite vs alternate nodes | Separate simple vs compound leaves | Inspect margins, hairs & stipules
visual: Conceptual botanical diagram illustrating opposite versus alternate leaf nodes and simple versus compound leaflet stems.
action: Verify node arrangement first—opposite (paired across the stem), alternate (staggered), whorled (three or more per node), or basal rosette—checking multiple healthy stems. @B20
action: Locate the axillary bud at the stem junction to separate a true simple leaf from leaflets on a compound leaf, preventing confusion between poison ivy, ash, and elderberry. @B12,B20
action: Document leaf margins (smooth/entire, toothed/serrated, lobed), venation (parallel, pinnate, palmate), and presence of milky or clear sap without tasting. @B10,B20
failure: Miscounting compound leaflets as opposite simple leaves misidentifies poison ivy, poison sumac, or buckthorn. @B12,B15,B20
verify: Node arrangement, bud location, margin type, venation, and sap color are documented cleanly with a ruler. @B20
stop: Stop vegetative identification when frost, browsing, or immaturity removes diagnostic leaves and buds. @B20
reviewer: Plant morphologist and botanist

Z05-P05 | Flower Symmetry, Inflorescence Types and Apiaceae Umbel Stop Rule | New manuscript (Z05 P19-C plant morphology)
objective: Identify major floral structures and enforce a mandatory stop gate on white- or yellow-umbel carrot-family (Apiaceae) plants.
applies: Spring through autumn flowering herbaceous and woody plant identification.
equipment: 10x hand lens, regional key, camera (hands-off unknown umbels).
diagram: Identify radial vs bilateral flowers | Flag compound umbel clusters | Enforce Apiaceae hemlock stop rule
visual: Conceptual botanical plate highlighting compound umbel rays and chambered root cutaway of lethal Apiaceae species.
action: Record floral symmetry (radial vs bilateral), petal/sepal counts, and cluster form (spike, raceme, panicle, composite head, or flat-topped umbel) using a hand lens. @B20
action: Treat every compound umbel (umbrella-like spoke cluster of small white or yellow flowers typical of Apiaceae) as a critical hazard flag because lethal water hemlock (Cicuta), poison hemlock (Conium), and burn-causing wild parsnip (Pastinaca) belong to this family. @B11,B14
action: Enforce a strict no-foraging hold on wild carrot-family roots and stems in field manuals because minuscule accidental bites of hemlock cause rapid seizures and death. @B11,B24
failure: Assuming white flat-topped roadside umbels are harmless Queen Anne lace risks fatal water-hemlock or poison-hemlock poisoning. @B11
verify: Inflorescence type is verified with zero bare-skin contact on yellow umbels and strict stop gate on wild Apiaceae. @B11,B14,B20
stop: Stop immediately and do not touch or sample any unidentified compound-umbel plant in wetlands, ditches, or roadsides. @B11,B14
reviewer: Botanist and medical toxicologist

Z05-P06 | Fruit, Berry, Drupe and Aggregate Structure Diagnostics | New manuscript (Z05 P19-C plant morphology)
objective: Separate true berries, single-stone drupes, aggregate bramble fruits, and toxic fleshy arils through cross-section anatomy.
applies: Summer and autumn wild-fruit recognition in Minnesota woodlands and edges.
equipment: 10x hand lens, metric ruler, gloves when handling unknown berries.
diagram: Count seeds and check stone shape | Contrast aggregate vs smooth berry | Never judge safety by berry color
visual: Conceptual fruit-anatomy plate contrasting multi-drupelet clusters against single-pitted drupes and toxic crescent-seeded moonseed.
action: Distinguish aggregate clusters of tiny drupelets (Rubus brambles), single-pitted drupes (Prunus cherries), multi-seeded berries (Vaccinium blueberries), pomes (Amelanchier/Crataegus), and fleshy cups/arils (lethal Taxus yew). @B10,B16,B20
action: Reject color folklore completely: white, red, purple-black, and blue fruits all include severely toxic Minnesota species (baneberry, yew, buckthorn, nightshade, moonseed, pokeweed). @B10,B15,B24
action: Inspect seed count and shape visually (for example, single crescent-shaped pitted seed of lethal Canada moonseed versus pear-shaped seeds of wild grape) without putting unknown fruit in your mouth. @B10,B20
failure: Assuming dark purple berries are safe or mistaking moonseed vines for wild grapes causes severe alkaloid poisoning. @B10,B15
verify: Fruit type, calyx remnant, seed shape, parent leaf/stem traits, and consumption HOLD are logged together. @B10,B20
stop: Stop immediately if fruit lacks parent foliage, shows crescent seed or red aril cup, or grows in a protected SNA. @B10,B22
reviewer: Regional botanist and toxicologist

Z05-P07 | Underground Structures: Taproots, Rhizomes, Tubers and Bulbs | New manuscript (Z05 P19-C plant morphology)
objective: Recognize extreme misidentification and legal-digging risks associated with wild roots, tubers, and bulbs.
applies: Botanical study of perennial underground storage organs across Minnesota.
equipment: Flora key only; zero digging on public lands or protected flora sites.
diagram: Separate bulb, tuber & taproot anatomy | Respect public-land zero-digging laws | Flag lethal wetland/meadow roots
visual: Conceptual subterranean anatomy cutaway separating layered bulbs, chambered toxic water-hemlock roots, and starch tubers.
action: Distinguish layered bulbs (Allium vs toxic death camas/daffodil), horizontal rhizomes (cattail vs toxic blue flag iris), fleshy taproots (burdock vs lethal hemlock), and stem tubers. @B11,B20
action: Observe strict public-land prohibitions: Minnesota State Parks, SNAs, National Parks, and rare-plant statutes prohibit digging wild roots, bulbs, or corms. @B17,B22,B23
action: Treat subterranean wild food identification as the highest-risk botanical category because dead winter tops remove diagnostic foliage and lethal Cicuta/Zigadenus roots resemble benign tubers. @B11,B17,B24
failure: Digging unflowering roots in spring marshes or meadows risks fatal water-hemlock or death-camas ingestion and destroys rare native perennials. @B11,B17
verify: Zero public-land digging occurs, above-ground diagnostic structures remain intact, and consumption stays strictly withheld. @B17,B22,B24
stop: Stop immediately whenever above-ground flowers/foliage are missing or wetland/public-land digging bans apply. @B11,B17,B22
reviewer: Botanist and conservation officer

Z05-P08 | Seasonal Phenology Shifts and Life-Stage Identification Traps | New manuscript (Z05 P19-C phenology)
objective: Prevent life-stage errors caused by young spring shoots, summer fruiting shifts, and bare winter stems.
applies: Year-round plant and fungal field observation across Minnesota seasons.
equipment: Seasonal phenology notes, Bell Museum Atlas date histograms.
diagram: Match current season & life stage | Flag risky early spring shoots | Stop when key structures are absent
visual: Conceptual four-season phenology plate illustrating shoot emergence, flowering diagnostics, autumn fruit, and bare winter dormancy.
action: Account for dramatic morphological changes across life stages: emerging spring shoots of toxic false hellebore, blue flag iris, water hemlock, and pokeweed closely mimic benign greens before stems and flowers mature. @B10,B11,B20
action: Verify whether a species normally fruits or flowers in the current Minnesota month using regional phenology charts before proposing an identification. @B01,B20
action: Apply a strict stop condition during winter dormancy or early spring when missing flowers, leaves, or fruiting bodies leave key diagnostic traits unobservable. @B20,B24
failure: Identifying a young leafless shoot or frost-blackened autumn stalk from memory causes high-severity seasonal poisonings. @B10,B11,B20
verify: Observed life stage matches documented seasonal window and all required diagnostic characters are physically present. @B01,B20
stop: Stop identification immediately if the specimen is too immature, frost-damaged, or decayed to verify every key trait. @B20,B24
reviewer: Plant ecologist and botanist

Z05-P09 | Minnesota Biomes, Plant Communities and Habitat Screening | New manuscript (Z05 P19-C regional ecology)
objective: Use Minnesota four ecological provinces—Laurentian Mixed Forest, Eastern Broadleaf, Prairie Parkland, and Tallgrass Aspen—to frame habitat context without substituting habitat for anatomy.
applies: Regional orientation for Z05 field observation across Minnesota.
equipment: MN DNR Native Plant Communities reference, biome map.
diagram: Orient to Minnesota ecological biome | Flag toxic wetland & edge habitats | Never infer ID from habitat alone
visual: Conceptual habitat transect spanning boreal bog, upland hardwood forest, woodland edge, and disturbed meadow.
action: Use Minnesota ecological provinces and native plant communities to anticipate expected native flora, boreal bog toxins (bog laurel, poison sumac), and woodland fungi. @B01,B18
action: Recognize that invasive and toxic species cross ecological boundaries freely; finding a plant in a familiar habitat never confirms its identity. @B18,B19
action: Protect intact native prairie remnants, calcareous fens, and old-growth forests by observing without trampling or harvesting. @B17,B18,B22
failure: Assuming a wooded bog contains only safe northern berries overlooks toxic bog rosemary, leatherleaf, and poison sumac. @B18
verify: Habitat notes supplement—never replace—multi-character anatomical verification. @B18,B20
stop: Stop collecting in rare native plant communities, fens, or SNAs regardless of species abundance. @B17,B18,B22
reviewer: Minnesota plant ecologist

Z05-P10 | Foraging Law: State Parks, SNAs, National Parks and Rare Plants | New manuscript (Z05 P19-C collection law)
objective: Comply with strict federal and Minnesota statutory boundaries governing plant, berry, root, and mushroom gathering on public lands.
applies: All Minnesota public lands, preserves, and private parcels.
equipment: Current MN DNR park/SNA rules, NPS 36 CFR 2.1, rare-species list.
diagram: Check the land manager | Confirm site and activity rules | Leave uncertain material in place
visual: Conceptual regulatory boundary contrasting SNA/NPS/rare-plant zero-take bans with tightly limited State Park rules.
action: Treat collection on Minnesota SNAs as restricted unless the current site rule specifically allows the activity. Check the unit’s own authorization on National Park Service land. @B22,R08
action: Check the exact Minnesota land type. State parks and state trails have different personal-use exceptions. Do not dig or damage protected plants. @B23
action: Check current listed-species protections and permissions before any collection. This guide withholds consumption regardless of land status. @B17,B23
failure: Digging wild leeks/ramps or ginseng in a State Park or picking berries inside an SNA violates state conservation statutes. @B17,B22,B23
verify: Land category, rare-plant status, current statute check date, and private-land permission are verified prior to any study. @B17,B22,B23
stop: If land status, activity authorization or species protections are uncertain, leave the material in place. @B22,B23
reviewer: Minnesota DNR botanist and conservation officer

Z05-P11 | Site Contamination Exclusions: Roadsides, Sprays and Floodplains | New manuscript (Z05 P19-C environmental contamination)
objective: Exclude chemically contaminated sites even when a species is familiar, preventing herbicide, lead, and pathogen ingestion.
applies: Evaluating site cleanliness across urban, suburban, agricultural, and trailside locations.
equipment: Site history review, local water/spray notices.
diagram: Reject roadsides & utility corridors | Exclude sprayed turf & industrial lots | Watch floodwater & pet-waste edges
visual: Conceptual spatial exclusion map isolating roadway runoff, railway rights-of-way, sprayed agriculture, and industrial lots.
action: Exclude all road shoulders, highway ditches, railroad rights-of-way, utility corridors, manicured lawns, golf courses, and agricultural field edges where herbicides, road salts, and brake/exhaust heavy metals accumulate. @B19,R12
action: Exclude urban lots near pre-1978 structures, former industrial sites, mine tailings, and floodplains carrying sewage or industrial sediment—fungi and wetland roots concentrate heavy metals like lead, cadmium, and arsenic. @B19,R12
action: Exclude low trailside vegetation heavily soiled by domestic pet waste or wild-animal feces carrying parasite eggs. @B19,H16
failure: Collecting familiar greens or mushrooms from a sprayed ditch or urban lot causes chemical or heavy-metal poisoning despite correct taxonomy. @B19,R12
verify: Site-history screening rules out sprays, traffic runoff, industrial soil, and floodwater before any specimen assessment. @B19,R12
stop: Stop immediately and reject any specimen growing near roads, railways, sprayed yards, industrial sites, or polluted water. @B19,R12
reviewer: Environmental toxicologist and botanist

Z05-P12 | Rejecting Universal Edibility Test Folklore and Cooking Myths | New manuscript (Z05 P19-C safety gate)
objective: Explicitly dismantle dangerous survival folklore—skin/lip rub tests, animal-browsing myths, silver-spoon tests, and universal boiling myths.
applies: All wild plant and fungal safety education.
equipment: CDC MMWR toxicology reports, Poison Control guidance.
diagram: Reject lip/skin rub edibility tests | Reject animal-eating safety myths | Know toxins that survive boiling
visual: Conceptual correct-versus-incorrect plate contrasting scientific multi-character verification against lethal folk edibility tests.
action: Reject the so-called universal edibility test (rubbing unknown plants on wrists, lips, or tongue in timed steps) completely: tiny doses of water hemlock or amatoxin mushrooms cause delayed fatal organ failure without immediate lip burn. @B07,B11,B24
action: Reject wildlife-browsing myths ("if squirrels or deer eat it, humans can"), silver-spoon tarnish tests, cap-peeling rules, and garlic-cooking myths—animals tolerate toxins lethal to humans, and amanitin/gyromitrin/alkaloids fail every folk cue. @B07,B24
action: Reject the myth that boiling destroys all plant and mushroom toxins: amatoxins in lethal Amanita and Galerina species survive prolonged boiling, drying, and freezing completely intact. @B06,B07,B24
failure: Trusting a lip-rub test or boiling an unknown woodland mushroom directly causes fatal liver failure or neurotoxic seizures. @B06,B07,B11,B24
verify: Zero folklore tests appear in field decision-making, and unrecognized specimens remain strictly untouched. @B24
stop: Stop immediately if anyone proposes a taste test, skin-rub test, or folk-cooking shortcut on an unknown plant or mushroom. @B24
reviewer: Medical toxicologist, botanist, and mycologist

Z05-P13 | Natural Plant Toxins: Oxalates, Cyanogenic Compounds, Tannins and Alkaloids | New manuscript (Z05 P19-C phytotoxicology)
objective: Understand major classes of plant secondary compounds so raw-toxicity, dose, and preparation boundaries are never oversimplified.
applies: Phytotoxicology awareness across wild plants, seeds, bark, and roots.
equipment: USDA ARS Poisonous Plant Research and Poison Control reference.
diagram: Classify major phytotoxin families | Separate raw hazard from folklore | Keep processing on specialist HOLD
visual: Conceptual comparison of major phytotoxin classes—calcium oxalates, cyanogenic glycosides, condensed tannins, and lethal alkaloids.
action: Understand major plant toxin groups: insoluble calcium oxalate needles (jack-in-the-pulpit, skunk cabbage) cause instant oral swelling; cyanogenic glycosides (Prunus pits/wilted leaves, raw elderberry stems) release cyanide; condensed tannins (raw acorns) cause GI/renal irritation; and cardiac/neurotoxic alkaloids (Taxus, Cicuta, Conium, Solanum) can be rapidly fatal. @B10,B11,B21
action: Recognize that toxin concentrations vary dramatically by plant part, maturity, drought stress, frost damage, and genetics—safe fruit pulp can sit adjacent to toxic seeds, leaves, or roots. @B10,B21
action: Treat traditional ethnobotanical leaching or cooking mentions as historical context only—never as validated modern clinical preparation clearances—maintaining WITHHELD_HOLD. @B21,B24
failure: Assuming an entire plant is uniform because one part is historically noted leads to poisoning from toxic roots, wilted leaves, or pits. @B10,B21
verify: Part-specific toxicity and absence of validated home detoxification guarantees remain explicit across every profile. @B21,B24
stop: Stop and call Poison Control (1-800-222-1222) immediately for oral burning, swelling, vomiting, confusion, or heart symptoms after any plant contact. @B09,B10
reviewer: Phytotoxicologist and clinical toxicologist

Z05-P14 | Individual Allergies, Drug Interactions and Vulnerable Populations | New manuscript (Z05 P19-C clinical limits)
objective: Recognize that even correctly identified wild species can trigger severe allergic reactions, medication interactions, or pediatric/pregnancy harm.
applies: Risk boundaries for children, pregnant/nursing people, older adults, and people with allergies or chronic illness.
equipment: Minnesota Poison Control 1-800-222-1222, personal allergy/medication list.
diagram: Leave wild-food consumption on hold | Check individual risk with a clinician | Use emergency care for severe signs
visual: Conceptual clinical risk boundary separating population-level botanical notes from individual medical vulnerability.
action: Recognize that cross-reactive food and pollen allergies (such as ragweed/Asteraceae, birch-pollen/fruit, nut, or fungal sensitivities) can cause hives or life-threatening anaphylaxis even from familiar species. @B10,B21,M14
action: Apply heightened caution for young children (who have low body weight and put attractive berries in mouths rapidly), pregnant or nursing individuals, and people taking anticoagulants, cardiac, or renal medications. @B10,B21
action: Maintain strict WITHHELD_HOLD status across Z05 and never conduct unsupervised wild-food trials in remote locations far from emergency medical care. @B21,B24
failure: Assuming a botanical note applies safely to every person ignores fatal nut/pollen anaphylaxis, pediatric dose sensitivity, and drug interactions. @B10,B21
verify: Individual allergy, pediatric, pregnancy, and medication exclusions are respected alongside taxonomic HOLD gates. @B10,B21,B24
stop: Stop immediately and activate 911 for throat swelling, wheezing, widespread hives, dizziness, or pediatric ingestion of any unknown wild plant or mushroom. @B09,M14
reviewer: Clinical toxicologist and allergist

Z05-P15 | Multi-View Diagnostic Photography and Provenance Standards | New manuscript (Z05 P19-C imaging standards)
objective: Capture scientifically usable multi-view plant and fungal photographs with scale, locality, and rights documentation for expert review.
applies: Documenting field specimens cleanly for botanist or mycologist evaluation.
equipment: Camera, matte metric scale card, notebook, GPS.
diagram: Shoot habitat, habit & close details | Include underside, base & cm scale | Keep documentary vs diagnostic clear
visual: Conceptual four-view specimen photography protocol showing habitat, top/side morphology, underside/base, and scale/locality log.
action: Capture a matched multi-view series of the exact same individual specimen: (1) wide habitat context, (2) full plant or fruiting cluster, (3) perpendicular macro of diagnostic leaves/flowers/cap, and (4) underside/gills/stem-base or fruit cross-section. @B02,B05,B20
action: Place a readable metric scale bar in frame on close-ups and record exact date, county, substrate/host tree, elevation/habitat, photographer credit, and copyright license. @B02,B05
action: Keep documentary habitat photos strictly distinct from expert-validated diagnostic evidence, and never fabricate or mislabel generated illustrations as real photographs. @B02,B24
failure: Submitting a single top-down phone snapshot without scale, underside, or stem base prevents experts from confirming or excluding lethal lookalikes. @B02,B05
verify: All four matched views, metric scale, substrate/locality notes, and rights metadata are archived together. @B02,B05
stop: Stop relying on incomplete single-angle photos or uncredited internet images for any identification decision. @B02,B24
reviewer: Curatorial botanist, mycologist, and image-rights reviewer

Z05-P16 | Reconciling Historical B011 Botanical and Fungal Atlas Leads | Adapted historical material (B011 atlas archive lead register)
objective: Integrate historical B011 botanical and fungal lead pointers cleanly under modern P19-A/B voucher, rights, and consumption HOLD gates.
applies: Editorial lineage bridging frozen Track B B011 leads to the Z05 atlas.
equipment: Frozen B011 crosswalk record, z05-taxonomic-register.tsv, photo-requirements.tsv.
diagram: Audit historical B011 species leads | Bind each to current Latin taxonomy | Keep zero fabricated photo plates
visual: Conceptual lineage governance plate showing historical B011 leads routed through taxonomic register, open photo queue, and specialist HOLD.
action: Preserve the historical B011 crosswalk finding accurately: zero diagnostic atlas specimen plates were acquired in B011—only preliminary research leads existed. @B01,B02
action: Map relevant Upper Midwest taxa into the controlled Z05 taxonomic register with accepted binomials, authorities, lookalikes, and open authentic diagnostic photography requirements. @B02,B03,B05
action: Maintain permanent WITHHELD_HOLD consumption status and zero fabricated specialist approvals across every B011-linked and newly authored Z05 profile. @B24
failure: Treating historical lead spreadsheets as verified field identification plates bypasses taxonomy, lookalike screening, and image rights. @B02,B24
verify: Every B011 lead is traceable, cleanly separated from acquired imagery, and governed by Z05 taxonomic and HOLD gates. @B02,B24
stop: Stop promoting any B011-linked species profile to approved publication without verified diagnostic photos and qualified human sign-off. @B02,B24
reviewer: Archival editor, botanist, and mycologist

Z05-P17 | Broadleaf Plantain (Plantago major) Morphological Profile | New manuscript (Z05 P19-C regional atlas)
objective: Document diagnostic traits of Plantago major and contrasting lookalikes under permanent consumption HOLD.
applies: Compacted trails, clearings, disturbed ground; Spring-Autumn (Basal rosette & flower spike).
equipment: Regional references, accession information and non-contact photographs or notes, where safe.
diagram: Check Plantago major traits | Contrast Hosta spp. | Keep WITHHELD_HOLD
visual: Conceptual botanical study plate with draft cues for Plantago major contrasting Hosta spp. with HOLD banner.
action: Compare the working name Plantago major (L.; syn. Common plantain) against a current regional key and accession. Unverified draft cues: Broad oval hairless basal leaves with 5-7 strong parallel arcuate ribs; fibrous stringy petioles; tall leafless cylindrical seed spike. @B01,B03,B20
action: Use the comparisons and draft traits in the taxonomic register for qualified regional review. Do not use any single feature, comparison, smell or image to rule out a toxic species. @B02,B20,B24
action: Separate recognition from eating (Site-spray contamination high on lawns; consumption withheld pending review); keep WITHHELD_HOLD and check site and species protections before any specialist fieldwork. @B19,B21,B24
failure: Single-trait guessing or common names confuse Plantago major with Hosta spp., risking poisoning. @B03,B10,B24
verify: Keep the identification provisional until the voucher, region, life stage and independent diagnostic characters are reviewed by the qualified specialist. @B02,B20
stop: Keep consumption on WITHHELD_HOLD. For suspected ingestion call U.S. Poison Help at 1-800-222-1222 promptly. Call 911 for collapse, seizure, breathing trouble or inability to wake. @B09,M15
reviewer: Regional botanist and toxicologist

Z05-P18 | Nettle Records: Urtica dioica and Native Urtica gracilis Naming | New manuscript (Z05 P19-C regional atlas)
objective: Document diagnostic traits of Urtica dioica and contrasting lookalikes under permanent consumption HOLD.
applies: Moist rich floodplains, woodland edges, stream banks; Spring-Summer (Vegetative & flowering stems).
equipment: Regional references, accession information and non-contact photographs or notes, where safe.
diagram: Compare taxonomic treatments | Check a regional voucher | Keep consumption on hold
visual: Conceptual plate for comparing voucher determinations and regions across nettle records.
action: Check which treatment and specimen record apply before using the name. Kew accepts Urtica gracilis as a distinct name and lists older Urtica dioica infraspecific names. Confirm the local voucher and current determination. @B25,B02
action: Document the example without touching stinging hairs. Compare leaf arrangement, stem and flowering traits to a current regional flora; no one trait establishes identification. @B02,B13,B20
action: Separate recognition from eating (Raw stinging hairs cause painful contact urticaria; old plants form irritating cystoliths; consumption withheld); keep WITHHELD_HOLD and check site and species protections before any specialist fieldwork. @B19,B21,B24
failure: Single-trait guessing or common names confuse Urtica dioica with Laportea canadensis, risking poisoning. @B03,B10,B24
verify: Keep the identification provisional until the voucher, region, life stage and independent diagnostic characters are reviewed by the qualified specialist. @B02,B20
stop: Keep consumption on WITHHELD_HOLD. For suspected ingestion call U.S. Poison Help at 1-800-222-1222 promptly. Call 911 for collapse, seizure, breathing trouble or inability to wake. @B09,M15
reviewer: Regional botanist and toxicologist

Z05-P19 | Common Dandelion (Taraxacum officinale) Morphological Profile | New manuscript (Z05 P19-C regional atlas)
objective: Document diagnostic traits of Taraxacum officinale and contrasting lookalikes under permanent consumption HOLD.
applies: Open meadows, fields, disturbed turf; Spring-Autumn (Basal rosette & flowering scape).
equipment: Regional references, accession information and non-contact photographs or notes, where safe.
diagram: Check Taraxacum officinale traits | Contrast Hypochaeris radicata | Keep WITHHELD_HOLD
visual: Conceptual botanical study plate with draft cues for Taraxacum officinale contrasting Hypochaeris radicata with HOLD banner.
