Z05-P95 | Fungal Anatomical Cutaway and Separate Mycological Schema | New manuscript (Z05 P19-B qualified workflow)
objective: Apply a dedicated mycological anatomy checklist—cap, fertile underside, stipe, ring, basal volva, spore print, and substrate—separate from plant keys.
applies: All macrofungi observation and voucher documentation in Minnesota.
equipment: MyCoPortal / regional mycology key, metric scale, dual black/white spore-print paper, wax/paper bag (never sealed plastic).
diagram: Inspect cap, gills/pores & stipe | Excavate intact base for volva | Record spore print & substrate
visual: Conceptual fungal anatomical cutaway labeling cap cuticle, gill attachment, annulus ring, stipe, and soil-level basal volva cup.
action: Document fungi using the dedicated fungal schema: cap surface/margin, fertile underside (gills, pores, teeth, or smooth) and gill attachment (free, adnate, decurrent), stem texture, partial-veil ring (annulus), and intact base (volva or bulb). @B05
action: Always inspect the intact basal structure carefully—never cut a mushroom above the soil line and assume no volva exists—and record substrate (soil, deadwood, conifer vs hardwood host) plus dual-color spore print. @B05,B06,B07
action: Recognize that macroscopic photos alone cannot separate many cryptic or toxic fungal complexes; keep consumption status strictly WITHHELD pending qualified mycologist verification. @B05,B06,B24
failure: Snapping off a mushroom cap above ground leaves behind a diagnostic basal volva, causing lethal Amanita species to mimic harmless edible mushrooms. @B05,B06,B07
verify: All six fungal anatomical structures plus substrate and spore-print data are logged with explicit consumption HOLD. @B05,B24
stop: Stop identification immediately on immature button stages, decayed specimens, or any mushroom missing an intact base or spore print. @B05,B06,B24
reviewer: Qualified mycologist

Z05-P96 | Spore-Print Collection and Fungal Substrate Documentation | New manuscript (Z05 P19-C mycology workflow)
objective: Record diagnostic spore-deposit color on split black-and-white paper alongside exact host-tree and substrate context.
applies: Documenting mature gilled, pored, and toothed mushrooms for mycological study.
equipment: Half-black/half-white clean paper card, glass cover, wax/paper bag, notebook.
diagram: Place mature cap fertile-side down | Use half-black & half-white paper | Log exact wood or soil substrate
visual: Conceptual spore-print plate showing split black/white deposit card alongside wood-versus-soil substrate documentation.
action: Collect a spore print by resting a mature fresh cap fertile-side down across both black and white halves of a clean card under a draft shield for 2–12 hours so white, cream, pink, green, rusty-brown, or black spores show clearly. @B05
action: Record exact substrate and nearby host trees—soil/duff versus living or dead hardwood versus conifer wood—because lookalike pairs like honey mushrooms (white spores) and deadly Galerina (rusty-brown spores) grow on identical deadwood. @B05,B08
action: Treat a spore print as one necessary character among many—never as a standalone edibility proof, because lethal Amanita species and safe mushrooms both drop white spores. @B05,B06,B24
failure: Relying on gill color instead of a true spore print confuses green-spored toxic Chlorophyllum molybdites or rusty-spored Galerina marginata with edible lookalikes. @B05,B08
verify: True spore-deposit color on black/white paper, gill attachment, and substrate/host tree are logged together under HOLD. @B05
stop: Stop identification if no spore deposit forms, specimen is moldy/decayed, or young veiled gills obscure maturity. @B05,B24
reviewer: Qualified mycologist

Z05-P97 | Fungal Decay, Heavy-Metal Bioaccumulation and Raw Cooking Warnings | New manuscript (Z05 P19-C fungal toxicology)
objective: Enforce strict hygiene against decayed sporocarps, sealed-plastic bacterial spoilage, heavy-metal uptake, and raw wild-mushroom toxicity.
applies: All macrofungi observation, collection law, and toxicology education.
equipment: Breathable paper/wax bags for voucher study only; zero sealed plastic bags.
diagram: Reject old, buggy, or wet fungi | Never seal fungi in plastic bags | Never eat raw wild mushrooms
visual: Conceptual failure-mode diagram showing bacterial spoilage inside sealed plastic bags and fungal heavy-metal uptake from urban/roadside soil.
action: Never store study or voucher mushrooms in sealed plastic bags because trapped moisture triggers rapid bacterial decomposition and secondary GI toxins even in non-toxic species. @B05,B08
action: Recognize that wild fungi bioaccumulate heavy metals (lead, cadmium, arsenic, mercury) and herbicides from urban parks, lawns, roadsides, and old orchards, and that many species (including true morels) contain heat-labile toxins when raw. @B08,B19
action: Remember equally that cooking NEVER destroys heat-stable lethal toxins such as amatoxins (Amanita, Galerina) or orellanine (Cortinarius); all Z05 fungal consumption remains strictly WITHHELD_HOLD. @B06,B07,B24
failure: Bagging damp mushrooms in plastic or tasting raw wild mushrooms causes severe gastrointestinal poisoning, while cooking lethal amatoxin fungi provides zero protection. @B06,B07,B08
verify: Breathable paper handling, clean non-urban site screening, and permanent consumption HOLD are upheld. @B08,B19,B24
stop: Stop handling decayed, slimy, or maggot-ridden fungi and call 1-800-222-1222 immediately for any raw or cooked wild-mushroom ingestion with symptoms. @B08,B09
reviewer: Qualified mycologist and food toxicologist

