Z05-P133 | Suspected Mushroom Poisoning and Delayed Symptoms | New manuscript (Z05 P19-C clinical toxicology)
objective: Recognize that absence or improvement of symptoms cannot establish safety after suspected mushroom ingestion.
applies: Suspected wild mushroom ingestion in the U.S., including when the person feels well.
equipment: Phone/911, Minnesota Poison Control 1-800-222-1222, clock to record exact ingestion time.
diagram: Call Poison Help promptly | Report exposure time and changes | Call 911 for emergency signs
visual: Decision diagram distinguishing emergency activation from Poison Help. No symptom-onset threshold is used.
action: Record the time of possible ingestion and any symptoms and call Poison Control promptly. Severe mushroom poisoning can have delayed symptoms. @B06,B09
action: Do not interpret a symptom-free interval or temporary improvement as recovery. Poison specialists assess the exposure using the clinical course and identity evidence. @B07,B09
action: Describe changes in condition to the poisoning specialist. Use 911 for collapse, seizures, difficulty breathing or inability to wake. Do not try to identify a toxin from the timing alone. @M15,B09
failure: Waiting for symptoms or treating temporary improvement as clearance can delay care. @B06,B07,B09
verify: Get the appropriate help promptly. Report the suspected exposure time and symptom changes if known. @B09,M15
stop: Call Poison Help promptly after suspected ingestion. Call 911 for collapse, seizure, difficulty breathing or inability to wake. @B09,M15
reviewer: Medical toxicologist and qualified mycologist

Z05-P134 | Report Existing Evidence After Suspected Plant or Mushroom Exposure | New manuscript (Z05 P19-C clinical workflow)
objective: Share what you already know with Poison Control or responders without delaying care or risking a new exposure.
applies: Immediately following any suspected toxic plant, berry, root, or wild mushroom ingestion.
equipment: Phone and existing exposure information, if safely available.
diagram: Call for help promptly | Describe evidence already available | Leave collection decisions to responders
visual: Four-step decision sequence prioritizing contact, existing information and responder instructions.
action: Call Poison Control promptly. Tell them what is already safely available, such as a photo or food remnant, and follow the specialist’s instructions. @B09,M16
action: Use photographs you can take safely without touching suspect material, revisiting a risky location or delaying care. @B02,M16
action: Leave collection and transport decisions to Poison Control or responders. Do not expose another person to obtain a specimen. @B09,M16
failure: Searching for a new specimen can delay care or expose another person. @B09,M16
verify: Confirm that Poison Control or responders have the information already available. Follow their instructions. @B09,M16
stop: Call 911 for collapse, seizure, breathing trouble or inability to wake. Do not gather samples at the expense of care or safety. @B09,M15
reviewer: Medical toxicologist and emergency mycology consultant

Z05-P135 | Poison Exposure Recognition and Emergency Referral (1-800-222-1222 / 911) | New manuscript (Z05 P19-C emergency referral)
objective: Provide clear, fail-closed layperson action steps and immediate escalation for suspected plant, berry, root, or mushroom poisoning.
applies: All suspected toxic ingestions, pediatric mouthing incidents, or severe plant-smoke/dermal exposures.
equipment: Phone and known substance, timing or route details if safely available.
diagram: Call 911 for emergency signs | Call Poison Help for other suspected exposures | Follow responder instructions
visual: Decision diagram distinguishing emergency activation from Poison Help after a suspected poison exposure.
action: Call 911 immediately for collapse, seizure, breathing trouble, or inability to wake. Follow dispatcher instructions and do not put anything in the person’s mouth. @B09,M15
action: For suspected ingestion without a life-threatening sign, call U.S. Poison Help at 1-800-222-1222 promptly. Do not wait for symptoms. @B09
action: Follow Poison Control or EMS for substance- and route-specific advice. Avoid home remedies, induced vomiting or new exposure while waiting. @B09,M15
failure: Inducing vomiting or waiting for symptoms can risk harm and delay help. @B09
verify: Use the appropriate emergency or poison line promptly and report the known details. @B09,M15
stop: For collapse, seizure, breathing trouble or inability to wake, call 911 immediately. For other suspected poison exposure get prompt Poison Control advice. @B09,M15
reviewer: Clinical medical toxicologist and EMS director
